The county attorney shall be the legal advisor and representative of the county and in such capacity shall:
(1) Advise the board of commissioners, county administrator and constitutional officers, when requested, upon all legal questions arising in the conduct of county business;
(2) Prepare or revise ordinances when so requested by the board of commissioners;
(3) Give opinions upon any legal matter or question submitted by the board of commissioners or the county administrator;
(4) Attend or have an assistant attend all regular or special meetings of the board of commissioners for the purpose of giving any legal advice requested by its members;
(5) Prepare or review for execution all contracts and instruments to which the county is a party and approve, as to form, all bonds required to be submitted to the county;
(6) Prepare, when authorized by the board of commissioners, all complaints filed, and appear in any court in the prosecution of every case to protect the rights of the county; see to the full enforcement of all judgments or decrees rendered or entered in favor of the county;
(7) Defend suits and actions at law or equity brought against the county unless otherwise directed by the board of commissioners,
(8) Make immediate report to the board of commissioners on the outcome of any litigation in which the county has an interest;
(9) Keep complete and accurate records of the following, which records shall remain the property of the county:
(a) All suits in which the county had or has an interest, giving the names of the parties; the nature of the action; the disposition of the case; its status, if pending; and the briefs of counsel, and
(b) All written opinions prepared by the county attorney and all certificates or abstracts of titles furnished by the county attorney to the county, or any department or official thereof; and
(10) Render such other legal services as may be required by the board of commissioners.
(Code 1979, § 1-3-4)
Cross reference— County attorney, appointment and compensation, § 1-3-4.

